WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.437
Here's why this matters.

2
00:00:01.437 --> 00:00:04.274
A modernized estate
isn't judged at cutover;

3
00:00:04.274 --> 00:00:07.425
it's judged the first Monday
someone can't sign in.

4
00:00:07.425 --> 00:00:11.638
And the most dangerous routine action
on the help desk is a single button.

5
00:00:11.638 --> 00:00:15.881
Requiring a user to re-register
for MFA wipes their methods,

6
00:00:15.881 --> 00:00:18.825
and whoever signs in
next sets up new ones.

7
00:00:18.825 --> 00:00:23.262
So a reset done on an unverified
request doesn't fix an account;

8
00:00:23.262 --> 00:00:25.263
it hands it to whoever asked.

9
00:00:25.263 --> 00:00:29.350
Attackers know this, and they call
help desks to do exactly that.

10
00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:32.409
Federal guidance agrees:
recovery is a bypass,

11
00:00:32.409 --> 00:00:36.113
and it needs high friction and
a notice to the real owner.

12
00:00:36.113 --> 00:00:39.875
So this book treats every
reset as an account transfer.

13
00:00:39.875 --> 00:00:42.500
Next, the model that makes that workable.
